    "https://careers.usc.edu.au/jobs/gbv-code-implementation-lead-unisc-sunshine-coast-qld-australia": {
      "title": "GBV Code Implementation Lead",
      "description": "GBV Code Implementation Lead - UniSC Sunshine Coast, QLD, Australia Contact Us Candidate login Explore Opportunities Academic jobs Professional jobs Casual jobs Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander jobs Recruitment process Employer information Applicant Settings Kiosk mode < BACK TO EXPLORE OPPORTUNITIES GBV Code Implementation Lead UniSC Sunshine Coast, QLD, Australia, 4556 Individual contributor L8 $118,986 - $134,756 +17% super Office of the Deputy Vice-Chancellor (Academic) Part-Time Add to favourites View favourites Office of the Deputy Vice Chancellor (Academic) | UniSC Sunshine Coast, Sippy Downs Level 8, $118,986 - $134,756 p.a., plus up to 17% employer superannuation Part-time 0.8FTE, fixed-term appointment until December 2027 The Opportunity At UniSC, we're committed to creating a safe, respectful and inclusive work and study environment where everyone can thrive. As part of this commitment, we're implementing our obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Respond to Gender-based Violence. This role will lead the coordinated delivery of that work across multiple portfolios, functions and stakeholder groups. Reporting to the Deputy Vice-Chancellor (Academic), you'll operate with a high degree of autonomy while bringing together expertise from Student Services, People and Culture, Governance and Risk Management, Information Technology Services, Legal, Marketing and Communications, and academic areas. Your focus will be on coordinating implementation, managing risk and dependencies, continuing the establishment of governance and assurance arrangements, and supporting a structured transition of ongoing responsibilities into business-as-usual operations. This is an ideal opportunity for an experienced program leader who enjoys navigating complexity, influencing across diverse stakeholder groups and delivering outcomes that create lasting organisational impact. You will lead the university’s implementation program, bringing together people, systems and processes to deliver UniSC’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Respond to Gender-based Violence; build alignment across diverse stakeholder groups, establishing clear governance, accountability, reporting and assurance arrangements that support sustainable outcomes; drive delivery and manage complexity, overseeing implementation plans, risks, dependencies, evidence requirements and system readiness across multiple portfolios; and embed long-term capability and ownership, supporting organisational change and the successful transition of processes, systems and responsibilities into business-as-usual operations. About you You're an accomplished project or program professional who thrives in complex environments and enjoys bringing people together to achieve shared outcomes. You can see the bigger picture while maintaining a strong focus on delivery, governance and accountability. You may have built your experience in higher education, government, health, community services, consulting or another large, regulated organisation. What matters most is your ability to coordinate complex work programs, engage stakeholders, navigate competing priorities and deliver sustainable outcomes. You'll bring excellent judgement, strong communication skills and a collaborative approach that enables you to influence without direct authority and build confidence across diverse stakeholder groups. You will possess: Postgraduate qualifications, progress towards postgraduate qualifications, or an equivalent combination of relevant experience and education/training. Significant experience managing complex, multi-stakeholder projects or programs within a large organisation. Demonstrated success coordinating cross-functional initiatives and building productive stakeholder relationships. Experience establishing governance, compliance, reporting and assurance frameworks that support organisational and regulatory objectives. Strong capability in project planning, risk management, dependency management and delivery oversight. Experience working with data, reporting processes, systems implementation or process integration activities. Outstanding written and verbal communication skills, including preparing reports, briefings and recommendations for senior stakeholders. Experience supporting organisational change and embedding sustainable business-as-usual practices. Desirable Knowledge of gender-based violence prevention and response, trauma-informed approaches, or related policy and regulatory frameworks. Experience delivering initiatives within higher education, government or another complex regulated environment. Don't meet every requirement? At UniSC, we understand that your work and life experiences can be just as valuable. If you're excited about this role but your CV doesn't align perfectly with our position description, we encourage you to apply anyway. You may be just the right candidate for this or one of our many other opportunities. UniSC is an equal opportunity employer, committed to diversity and inclusion. We encourage applications from all backgrounds, including those with disabilities, women in STEM, First Nations Peoples, and individuals of diverse cultures, genders, abilities, and experiences. We value achievement relative to opportunity and encourage your commentary on your achievements in this context. For additional support during the application process, please contact us at unisc-careers@usc.edu.au or call +61 7 5430 2830. How to apply All applications must be lodged through our website. To apply please visit our website at: Careers at UniSC Applications for this position close midnight, Sunday 18 October 2026. Position Description: 494888_GBV Code Implementation Lead.pdf Your completed application must include: a resume, and a response to selection criteria. Noting that within the application form, we will request a statement addressing each of the selection criteria contained within the position description. Further information regarding how to prepare your application is available on our website: Join our team. Have questions or want to know more? For a confidential discussion, please contact: Professor Michael Wilmore Deputy Vice Chancellor (Acaemic) Email: DVCAcademic@usc.edu.au We would love the opportunity to get to know you better, but we know it works both ways. To get to know a little more about us, visit us at: Welcome to UniSC and LinkedIn Life UniSC values a diverse and talented workforce and invites applications from candidates who already have legal work rights in Australia. UniSC does not accept unsolicited applications. For agency inquiries, contact unisc-careers@usc.edu.au Discover UniSC Our Culture & Benefits: Our collaborative, passionate and welcoming culture is what really sets us apart. Modern teaching & research facilities. Professional development and career pathway opportunities. We are a proud recipient of the prestigious Athena SWAN Bronze Award, granted as part of the Science in Australia Gender Equity (SAGE) initiative. Flexible work arrangements & generous leave options. Salary packing options + 17% Super. EAP, fitness passport & discounted private health. Video Player is loading. Play Video Play Skip Backward Skip Forward Mute Current Time 0:00 / Duration 1:47 Loaded : 0.00% 0:00 Stream Type LIVE Seek to live, currently behind live LIVE Remaining Time - 1:47 1x Playback Rate Chapters Chapters Descriptions descriptions off , selected Subtitles subtitles settings , opens subtitles settings dialog subtitles off , selected Audio Track Picture-in-Picture Fullscreen This is a modal window. Beginning of dialog window. Escape will cancel and close the window. Text Color White Black Red Green Blue Yellow Magenta Cyan Opacity Opaque Semi-Transparent Text Background Color Black White Red Green Blue Yellow Magenta Cyan Opacity Opaque Semi-Transparent Transparent Caption Area Background Color Black White Red